<p>French prosecutors in Paris have set up a special team of magistrates to review evidence in the so-called “Epstein files”. Prosecutors also plan to take a fresh, thorough look at the case of Jean-Luc Brunel, a French modelling agency executive described as a close Epstein associate. Brunel died in custody in 2022 while facing charges in France linked to alleged sexual violence against minors.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/pdpk0YMnhNPUUruXn4nGNWntoK4AlLbYs1bP4ee1aSFSLkqN3ImNARXyJ6vUrXCl8j3vRbq_vC5V0rrnYcjN3_emDfOADmsGTiJAEzZVbpo84TILkiz-bILDBsqXNRwCReeFtZYqGgtayiBB6WSvrYjFaVzDqAzt1VLWxg_7KMk13oq5EFomhjSAGCCbF96A_FRXjhS7Rz7-iQ' alt='The Santé prison in Paris where Jean-Luc Brunel, an Epstein associate who died in 2022, was held.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Document release context: The move follows a recent release of documents by US authorities, which has renewed scrutiny of Epstein’s network and possible links to prominent French figures.</li><li>French-nationals focus: The magistrates will analyse evidence that could implicate French nationals in crimes connected to Epstein’s case.</li><li>Systematic review: Prosecutors say the dedicated team will systematically examine the “Epstein files” to identify any further possible offences.</li><li>New complaints and diplomat: The Paris prosecutor’s office says it is examining three new complaints linked to the files, including one involving a diplomat.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Paris prosecutor’s office: It has set up a dedicated team of magistrates to systematically analyse material from the “Epstein files” and identify any further possible offences. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/france-assembles-magistrate-team-to-examine-epstein-files/a-75975774'>DW</a>)</li><li>Document release context: Reporting says the release of documents by US authorities has refocused attention on Epstein’s network and sparked scrutiny of possible ties to prominent French figures. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/14/french-prosecutors-to-set-up-special-team-to-review-epstein-files'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Complainants in new filings: The Paris prosecutor’s office says it is reviewing three newly filed complaints linked to the files, signalling fresh allegations for investigators to assess. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/14/world/europe/france-epstein-files.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/france-assembles-magistrate-team-to-examine-epstein-files/a-75975774'>France assembles magistrate team to examine &#x27;Epstein files&#x27;</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/14/world/europe/france-epstein-files.html'>French Prosecutors to Investigate Diplomat as Part of Epstein Probe</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/french-prosecutors-announce-special-team-for-epstein-files'>French prosecutors announce special team for Epstein files</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/14/french-prosecutors-to-set-up-special-team-to-review-epstein-files'>French prosecutors to set up special team to review Epstein files</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/france-sets-up-special-team-to-review-evidence-tied-to-jeffrey-epstein-13979890.html'>France sets up special team to review evidence tied to Jeffrey Epstein</a> - firstpost.com</li></ul>